course: world
lesson: 21
title: Parts of a plant and what each does
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A flowering plant is like a small factory with different parts doing different jobs.
explain: The roots hold the plant in the soil and take in water and minerals; the stem holds the plant up and carries water up and food around through thin tubes.
explain: The leaves are the kitchens of the plant: they use sunlight to make food for the whole plant.
explain: The flower makes seeds, and the fruit protects the seeds and helps them travel.
explain: Put a white carnation in a glass of water with blue ink: after a day its petals turn blue, because the stem carried the coloured water all the way up.
fact: Roots anchor a plant in the soil so wind and rain cannot easily pull it out.
fact: Roots take in water and minerals from the soil through tiny root hairs.
fact: The stem holds up the leaves and flowers so they can reach the light.
fact: The stem has thin tubes that carry water up from the roots and food from the leaves to other parts.
fact: Leaves make food for the plant using sunlight.
fact: Leaves have tiny holes, mostly on their underside, that let gases in and out and let water vapour escape.
fact: The flower is the part of the plant that makes seeds.
fact: The fruit grows from part of the flower and holds the seeds inside.
fact: A seed contains a tiny young plant and a store of food for it.
fact: Some plants store food in their roots, like carrots and sweet potatoes, or in underground stems, like potatoes.
fact: Plants do not take their food from the soil; they make it themselves in their leaves.
exercise: Which part of a plant takes in water from the soil? | The roots | The leaves | The flower | The fruit
exercise: Which part of a plant makes its food? | The leaves | The roots | The flower | The seed
exercise: What does the stem do? | It holds the plant up and carries water and food | It makes seeds for new plants | It soaks up water straight out of the air around it | It eats insects that land on it
exercise: Which part of a flowering plant makes seeds? | The flower | The deep root | The green leaf | The tall stem
exercise: What is the job of the fruit? | To protect the seeds and help them spread | To make food from sunlight | To take water from the soil | To hold the plant firmly down in the ground
exercise: What is inside a seed? | A tiny young plant and its store of food | Only water and some soil | A small stone that later grows | A fully formed flower, folded up very small
exercise: Which part of a plant do we eat when we eat a carrot? | The root | The stem | The leaf | The flower
exercise: A potato is a swollen part of which plant part? | An underground stem | A long, thin root hair | A fruit that fell off | A leaf bud under the soil
exercise: Why does a plant need roots besides taking in water? | To hold it firmly in the ground | To catch sunlight underground | To make seeds in the soil | To breathe out oxygen at night
exercise: What do root hairs do? | They take in water and minerals | They catch light for the plant | They make the flowers' colours | They scare away soil animals
exercise: Where does a plant get its food from? | It makes its food in its leaves | It eats food from the soil | It drinks food from the rain | It gets food from its flowers
exercise: Why do most stems grow upwards? | To lift the leaves towards the light | To get away from the cold soil | To keep the roots dry and warm | To stop animals from eating them
exercise: What leaves the plant through the tiny holes in its leaves? | Water vapour and gases | Seeds and yellow pollen | Soil and the minerals in it | Roots and little root hairs
exercise: Where are the tiny holes of a leaf mostly found? | On the underside of the leaf | On the top of the stem | On the tips of the roots | Inside the seeds
test: A carnation stands in water coloured with blue ink, and next day its petals are blue. What does this show? | The stem carries water up to the flower | Petals make their own blue paint | Flowers soak up the colour of the sky from the air | Roots turn water into food
test: A gardener cuts off all the leaves of a young plant. What will happen to it? | It cannot make food and will weaken | It will grow faster without leaves | It will take its food from the soil | Nothing, leaves are only decoration
test: A sweet potato is a swollen food store of its plant. Which part of the plant is it? | The root | The flower | The leaf | The stem
test: Which statement about plant food is true? | Plants make their own food | Plants eat soil to get their food | Plants get all their food from rain | Plants get their food from insects
test: After a storm, which tree is more likely to stay standing? | One with deep, spreading roots | One with large, bright flowers | One with very many fruits | One with thin, smooth leaves
test: A cherry contains a hard stone. What is the stone? | The seed protected by the fruit | A root that grows inside fruit | A stem that fell into the fruit | A leaf bud that has hardened
test: What would happen to a plant whose stem was crushed so its tubes broke? | Water could not reach its leaves | It would make more flowers | Its roots would grow leaves | It would make food faster
test: Why do seeds carry their own food store? | To feed the young plant before it has leaves | To feed the soil around the seed | To make the seed heavier, so it sinks into the soil | To attract animals that eat roots
test: Which part of a plant lets water vapour escape into the air? | Tiny holes in the leaves | The hard skin of the seeds | The tips of the roots | The centre of the flower
test: why do plants have roots anyway | To hold the plant in the ground and take in water | To make food from the sunlight | To make the seeds of the plant | To breathe in all the air it needs from under the ground
test: wots the flower even for | It makes the seeds | It makes the plant's food | It soaks up water from the rain | It holds the plant in the soil
test: Mia reckons plants eat soil to grow. right? | No, plants make their food from light, air and water | Yes, plants eat the soil through their roots | Yes, soil is the main food of all plants | No, plants eat the insects in the soil
test: Tom says a potato is a root like a carrot. true? | No, a potato is an underground stem | Yes, potatoes and carrots are both roots | Yes, a potato is a root with eyes | No, a potato is a fruit of the plant
exam: Identify the part of the plant principally responsible for the absorption of water and minerals. | The roots | The leaves | The flowers | The fruit
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the nutrition of plants? | Plants produce their own food within their leaves | Plants obtain their food entirely from the soil | Plants obtain their food from rainwater | Plants obtain their food from insects
exam: Identify the part of the plant from which seeds are produced. | The flower | The root system | The principal stem | The foliage leaf
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the potato. | It is an underground stem in which food is stored | It is a root that absorbs water | It is the fruit of the potato plant | It is a seed containing a young plant and its store of food
